* Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:
* Time complexity: O(n) because the loop runs n times doing constant-time operations.
* Space complexity: O(n) auxiliary space because a new vector of size n is created to store the answer.
* Why my approach works, or why it doesn't:
* Imagine nums is [0, 2, 1, 5, 3, 4]. When i is 0, nums[0] is 0, and nums[0] is 0, so ans gets nums[0] which is 0. When i is 1, nums[1] is 2, and nums[2] is 1, so ans gets 1. This correctly simulates the permutation mapping.

3. ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: New Array Simulation (Your Approach)

* Idea: Create a new result array of the same size, loop through each index from 0 to n - 1, and store nums[nums[i]] in the result.
* C++ code:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> buildArray(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> ans;
        for(int i=0; i<nums.size(); i++){
            ans.push_back(nums[nums[i]]);
        }
        return ans;
    }
};

```

* Time complexity: O(n)
* Space complexity: O(n) for the output array.
* What makes the next approach better: It allocates extra memory. If the interviewer asks for O(1) extra space, we can store two numbers inside a single integer using math.

Approach 2: In-Place Encoding (Optimal O(1) Space)

* Idea: Since all numbers are strictly less than n, we can store both the original number and the new number in the same integer slot using the formula value = old_value + (new_value % n) * n. Afterwards, divide every element by n to get the final answer.
* C++ code:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> buildArray(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            nums[i] = nums[i] + (nums[nums[i]] % n) * n;
        }
        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            nums[i] = nums[i] / n;
        }
        return nums;
    }
};

```

* Time complexity: O(n)
* Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because we modify the input array directly without extra memory.

4. WHERE I STAND

* Which approach did I use? You used Approach 1 (New Array Simulation).
* Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem? Interviewers expect Approach 1 first to test basic array simulation, and then as a follow-up, they ask for Approach 2 to see if you can achieve O(1) space.
* Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:
* Question: Can you solve this without using extra space?
* Answer: Yes, by encoding two values in one integer using modulo and division arithmetic since all values are less than n.

5. WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

* The key insight, in one sentence: When values are bounded by n, you can pack two numbers into one integer space using modulo and multiplication arithmetic.
* My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time: Pre-allocate vector size when output dimensions are known to avoid push_back overhead.
